**Concentration:** 25.0% Parfum — 7,500 µL concentrate in a 30.00 mL bottle  
**Concentrate target:** 7500 uL  
**Inventory path:** `../inventory.txt`  
**Inventory authority:** `inventory.txt` refreshed 2026-08-29; SHA-256 `6278A846882412F2F026171D0B4910C4613C35D6C7B6680BF4B98C923E4BB5D7`  
**Design authority:** direct user approval, 2026-08-28  

## Target Identity and Functional Architecture

The design borrows the useful convention of the 2025 Dior Homme Parfum study—one dominant floral object held in relief by transparent pressure and a fading wood background—without importing iris, leather, oud, vanilla, tonka, or a generic woody-amber genre. Dior’s public description supports the iris-over-woods reference grammar, while this formula translates that spatial idea into rose rather than attempting similarity to Dior Homme Parfum.

The object has five linked planes:

1. **Cold living surface:** steam-distilled Rose Otto, Nerol, Phenyl Ethyl Acetate, a restrained Linalool flash, and Rose Oxide. This is dew, cool petal edge, and the volatile upper face—not citrus cologne and not orange blossom.
2. **Velvet interior:** Rose de Mai Absolute, Phenethyl Alcohol, controlled Citronellol, low Geraniol, and PEDMC. This is the dense petal body. Citronellol and Geraniol are intentionally bounded to prevent geranium/citronella dominance.
3. **Rose-ketone depth:** Alpha Damascone, Damascone Beta, Damascenone, and Damascol each occupy a distinct metallic, plum-woody, bloom-amplifying, or rounded-fruity register. They are a transition system, not four ingredients offered as complexity evidence.
4. **Adhesive boundary:** Hexyl Salicylate forms the sheer outer film; Benzyl Salicylate forms the warmer inner cushion; Benzyl Benzoate supplies low-odor mass. This gradient keeps the petal object continuous from air to skin.
5. **Receding pressure field:** Virginia cedar supplies natural grain, trace Azarbre supplies far-depth warmth, Clearwood supplies clean earth shadow, and the ideal Ambrofix/current-build Ambrox Super register supplies restrained crystalline-mineral pressure. Benzyl Benzoate creates distance between those planes. Romandolide is the sole musk and serves only outward diffusion. None may become a freestanding base accord.

Hard exclusions are geranium/citronella effect, jam or Turkish delight, lipstick/iris/powder, soap/laundry musk, gourmand sweetness, oud/incense/leather/patchouli rose, Hedione takeover, citrus-cologne framing, and a generic amberwood drydown.

## TARGET / IDEAL FORMULA

This is the target’s normalized **active-odorant** architecture. “Active parts” deliberately does not mean bottle-stock volume. Citronellyl Acetate remains visible here as the one target-only gap so the ideal is not silently collapsed into what happens to be owned.

| Material | Active parts | Target-linked function | Selection logic |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Rose de Mai Absolute | 55.00 | velvet petal interior | waxier and denser than Rose Otto; selected over another synthetic rose alcohol for natural relational detail |
| Rose Essential Oil / Rose Otto | 30.00 | cold living upper rose | steam-distilled lift and spicy-citronellol motion; selected over neroli because the subject is rose, not orange blossom |
| Phenethyl Alcohol | 360.00 | continuous fresh-petal body | the least genre-shifting high-volume rose body; preferred to Rhodinol, which risks a loud geranium/citronella profile |
| Citronellol | 80.00 | soft natural rose volume | bounded below classical-heavy loading to avoid citronella dominance |
| Geraniol | 8.00 | bright rose articulation | deliberately low because the naturals already carry geraniol and the modeled ODT makes it easy to overstate |
| Nerol | 35.00 | sweet citrus-rose bridge | supplies petal brightness without importing a citrus accord |
| Phenyl Ethyl Dimethyl Carbinol | 40.00 | magnolia-clean petal thickness | softer, rounder rose body than more PEA alone |
| Phenyl Ethyl Acetate | 22.00 | fresh honeyed-fruity petal lift | chosen instead of a generic fruit ester because it remains chemically continuous with the PEA body |
| Linalool | 3.00 | brief volatile air | restrained; bergamot was rejected because it would frame the object as a citrus floral |
| Rose Oxide | 0.20 | cold metallic dew | exact surface cue; kept below an identifiable metallic-rose effect |
| Alpha Damascone | 0.70 | metallic rose brilliance | the sharper upper edge of the ketone depth module |
| Damascone Beta | 0.90 | plum-woody inner shadow | the darker fruit-to-wood transition |
| Damascenone | 0.05 | bloom amplification | catalytic rose bloom, not a jam note |
| Damascol | 1.50 | rounded rose-plum continuity | softens the alpha/beta damascone edges |
| Geranyl Acetate | 7.00 | leafy-fruity bloom transition | now owned neat; cleaner petal motion than more Geraniol and used as an exact transition rather than generic extra richness |
| Citronellyl Acetate | 8.00 | fresh pear-rose cohesion | target-only gap; prevents the alcohol core from feeling skeletal |
| Hedione | 60.00 | transparent internal air | standard Hedione selected alone; Hedione HC was rejected as redundant and more likely to pull toward white floral |
| Benzyl Salicylate | 110.00 | warm inner adhesion | appropriate here because the interior is explicitly velvet and dense, not sheer |
| Hexyl Salicylate | 55.00 | transparent outer adhesion | creates a lighter film above Benzyl Salicylate rather than duplicating its weight |
| Benzyl Benzoate | 40.65 | low-odor mass, fixation, and plane separation | selected where additional salicylate character would be excessive; the small increase prevents the added wood registers from becoming one opaque block |
| Cedarwood oil Virginia | 13.00 | natural dry grain behind the rose | introduces irregular wood texture at low level instead of a polished sandalwood co-star |
| Azarbre | 2.00 | distant warm cedar-amber depth | a trace far plane; deliberately below a freestanding amberwood effect |
| Clearwood | 8.00 | clean earthy receding shadow | selected over Patchouli EO to avoid a patchouli-rose identity |
| Ambrofix | 10.00 | smooth mineral pressure | selected over Amber Xtreme, Ambermax, and Amberwood F because the needed register is restrained crystalline pressure, not amber impact or warmth |
| Romandolide | 50.00 | outward diffusion | sole functionally exact musk; EB would thicken the petal, Habanolide would add redundant warmth, and laundry/polycyclic musks would change the genre |
| **Total active architecture** | **1000.00** |  |  |

