## Stock-Rebase and Dose-Floor Guard

- Every direct stock dose is at least 20 µL. The full-scale Azarbre dose is 20 µL of a deliberately prepared 10% w/w DEP working stock; at the 15 mL blind-arm scale it is 10 µL exactly, never a sub-10 µL direct dose.
- Prepare the Azarbre working stock by mass: **0.100 g neat Azarbre + 0.900 g DEP = 1.000 g at 10.0% w/w**. Label material, concentration basis, carrier, preparation date, and batch mass. Do not use it if it is visibly nonuniform or develops phase separation.
- The 10% w/w Rose de Mai and Rose Otto stocks are rebased to 200 and 90 µL active respectively. Their raw stock volumes must never be mistaken for neat natural mass.
- Both natural rows are historical physical-batch provenance only. Rose de Mai is depleted and Rose Otto is sensory-rejected; add neither material to this bottle or any future formula unless a refreshed authoritative inventory explicitly reinstates a new stock.
- The owned Ambrox Super stock is **5.000 g active in 15.00 mL total**, approximately 0.333 g/mL and labeled ~33% w/v in DEP:EtOH 53:47. A 60 µL dose therefore carries approximately **19.80 mg active**, or **2640 ppm w/w in the 7.50 g concentrate** under the formula’s density≈1 convention. Do not silently reinterpret this w/v stock as w/w or neat.
- The heterogeneous Ambrofix bottle remains owned but is not used in this current build. Its visible crystals and whole-bottle 7.27% w/w mass balance do not authorize pipetting or a sensory-equivalence claim.
- The 15 mL blinded-arm scale uses exactly one-half of every listed dose. Its minimum direct dose is 10 µL Linalool, so it also respects the floor without a new dilution.
- The Rose Otto carrier remains unrecorded. The 900 µL row is retained only as historical physical-batch provenance. The current lot is sensory-rejected, non-executable, and must not be used in a future formula; no stability or phase claim follows from the missing carrier label.

## Current Background Diagnostic — Modeled, Non-Release

The table below is a bounded local `FormulaState` comparison at 305 K using the executable Ambrox Super stock and the constant-total DEP replacement. It is not measured headspace, strict sensory OAV, a performance claim, or a hedonic result. Natural-composite values remain model floors and the Azarbre ODT remains unverified.

| Material | Active ppm in concentrate | ODT ppm / authority | Opening OAV | Modeled 4 h OAV | Diagnostic reading |
|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---|
| Cedarwood oil Virginia | 5333.3 | 0.007592 / derived effective composite-natural decomposition | 10.94 | 10.48 | plausible persistent natural grain; lot-specific intensity remains unmeasured |
| Azarbre | 266.7 | 0.002000 / unverified project threshold | 11.13 | 9.96 | plausible far-depth signal, but the ODT uncertainty makes this a conservative physical-test candidate rather than a numeric conclusion |
| Clearwood | 8000.0 | 0.010000 / unverified project threshold | 0.04 | 0.04 | modeled sub-threshold and accepted only as structural recession |
| Ambrox Super | 2640.0 | 0.000300 / derived, exact Leffingwell match in local verifier | 5.54 | 5.61 | modeled perceptible pressure below the cedar/Azarbre/Romandolide poles; physical smoothness and strength remain untested |
| Romandolide | 37333.3 | 0.004900 / peer-reviewed project record | 12.04 | 12.17 | sole modeled musk diffusion pole |
| Benzyl Benzoate | 40000.0 | 0.810000 / peer-reviewed project record | 0.0009 | 0.0009 | intentionally structural plane separation, not a perceptible character note |

The modeled base headspace share remains below 1% even though Cedarwood, Azarbre, Ambrox Super, and Romandolide each occupy a distinct background function. That is the reason not to add indiscriminate base mass: the testable opportunity is their relationship—natural grain, warm far depth, clean earth shadow, mineral pressure, and outward diffusion. Whether that relationship is more hedonic is reserved for the blinded arms below.

## Missing-Chemical Impact Gate

| Gap | Target role | Impact if omitted | Current-build response | Decision |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Citronellyl Acetate | pear-fresh rose cohesion | **Medium**: less polished linkage between Citronellol and the volatile surface | controlled Citronellol + Nerol + Phenyl Ethyl Acetate provide a narrower executable bridge | omit from current build; optional future controlled sidearm |
| Polysantol — user-confirmed out of stock | former single-tone warm sandalwood shadow | **Medium if merely deleted**: the rose could lose a warm back plane | target and current build are deliberately redesigned as low Virginia cedar + trace Azarbre + extra Benzyl Benzoate | exclude; this is a new layered-background hypothesis, not a claim of sensory equivalence and not an automatic repurchase request |
| Homogeneous Ambrofix dosing stock | ideal smoother mineral-pressure material | **Low-to-medium**: owned Ambrox Super may read crisper or more clinical even at the matched structural register | 60 µL of the owned ~33% w/v Ambrox Super stock plus 140 µL DEP preserves total raw volume and a subordinate modeled OAV | use the executable approximation for T1; retain Ambrofix only in TARGET/IDEAL and do not infer sensory equivalence |

**Missing-chemical gate result:** `NO_CRITICAL_MISSING_CHEMICAL_GAP`. Geranyl Acetate is present as the target-linked leafy-fruity transition. The Citronellyl Acetate omission affects realism refinement, while the Ambrox-for-Ambrofix substitution affects pressure texture; neither removes the recognizable rose architecture. Execution requires only that the Azarbre 10% w/w DEP preparation be made and recorded; safety and physical validation remain HOLDs.

## Purchase / Preparation Recommendation

**Immediate recommendation: no purchase is required for Trial T1 and do not repurchase Polysantol before the background trial.** Prepare 1.000 g of the Azarbre 10% w/w DEP working stock as specified above, then prepare the current-inventory build exactly as written with 60 µL of the owned Ambrox Super stock and 140 µL DEP. Do not use the heterogeneous Ambrofix bottle, and do not add Neroli, citrus, iris materials, vanilla/tonka, another musk, Amber Xtreme, or extra woods to “improve” a numerical score.

If T1 is clearly rose-identical but the surface-to-heart transition is still judged flat in the blinded test, buy **Citronellyl Acetate only** for a constant-total realism sidearm; Geranyl Acetate is already owned neat and is now in T1. Make no sub-10 µL direct additions: if a future sidearm requires one, prepare a verified working stock whose carrier matches the source bottle, then rebase total raw volume, active ppm, ODT/OAV, and every temporal window before testing.

## Mixing Order

Pour basket by basket, high to low raw uL within each basket:

1. **BASKET 1 — Always used:** Hedione (280).
2. **BASKET 3 — Woods:** Clearwood (60), Cedarwood oil Virginia (40), Azarbre 10% w/w in DEP (20).
3. **BASKET 4 — Wood modifiers and ambers:** Benzyl Salicylate (600), Hexyl Salicylate (300), Benzyl Benzoate (300), **60 µL Ambrox Super ~33% w/v**.
4. **BASKET 6 — Musks:** Romandolide (280).
5. **BASKET 7 — Muguet and lavender:** Linalool (20).
6. **BASKET 10 — Rose:** Rose de Mai Absolute (2000), PEA (1170), Rose Otto (900), Citronellol (400), PEDMC (220), Nerol (180), Phenyl Ethyl Acetate (120), Rose Oxide (100), Geraniol 10% (100), Damascol (75), Damascone Beta (45), Alpha Damascone (35), Geranyl Acetate (30), Damascenone (25).
7. **CARRIER / TECHNICAL:** **140 µL DEP**.

Stir after each basket. Add ethanol to 30.00 mL. Record actual mass/volume, appearance, and any phase behavior rather than assuming stability.

**Preparation note (unchanged):** Prepare and label Azarbre 10% w/w in DEP first. Confirm the prepared Azarbre stock and the owned Ambrox Super stock are visibly uniform before use; observation of uniformity is not a stability result. Do not use the heterogeneous Ambrofix bottle. Add the ketone module (Damascol, Damascone Beta, Alpha Damascone, Damascenone, Rose Oxide) from least to most potent stock, mixing between additions. Evaluate first at 48 hours and again after 14 days. These are comparison times, not evidence that maturation or stability is complete.

## Controlled Blinded Test Plan

Prepare three constant-total arms, each as **two independent 15 mL preparations**. Use one-half of every T1 dose for each 15 mL preparation, giving 3,750 µL concentrate plus 11.25 mL ethanol. This preserves the 10 µL direct-dose floor.

- **T1 — Full object:** formula exactly as written.
- **T2 — Cedar-only background:** replace the half-scale **10 µL Azarbre 10% w/w in DEP** dose with 10 µL DEP. This isolates the far-depth Azarbre trace while preserving carrier and total raw volume.
- **T3 — Background omission:** replace the half-scale **20 µL Cedarwood oil Virginia** dose with 20 µL Benzyl Benzoate, and replace the half-scale 10 µL Azarbre stock with 10 µL DEP. This removes the newly articulated wood background at constant total volume while retaining the rest of the pressure field.

Keep **30 µL Ambrox Super ~33% w/v plus 70 µL DEP** constant in every 15 mL arm. The historical half-scale instructions for Ambrofix—whether 100 µL from a nominal 30% stock or 100 µL from the heterogeneous 7.27% whole-bottle stock—are superseded and must not be executed. The arms become compoundable after the Azarbre working stock is prepared and recorded; skin testing remains on safety HOLD.

Assign six random codes after preparation. The evaluator should not know which two bottles are replicates. Use equal blotters, equal spray or measured application, the same room, and randomized presentation order. Evaluate at 0, 5, and 30 minutes, then 2, 4, and 8 hours. Do not begin skin testing until the safety/IFRA review is accepted for the intended use.

At each time point record, separately:

- rose identity strength and whether any non-rose genre becomes named first;
- realism of dew, fresh petal, velvet interior, honey/spice nuance, stem/leaf trace, and dry petal;
- front/back depth, edge definition, internal richness, and continuity of transitions;
- diffusion and persistence as observations, never inferred from modeled OAV;
- explicit drift flags: geranium/citronella, soap/laundry, jam/candy, powder/lipstick, citrus cologne, sandalwood, patchouli, or amberwood;
- hedonic preference only after identity and drift are scored, with ties allowed.

Advance T1 only if its two independent preparations agree directionally, it defeats T2 and T3 on rose-linked front/back depth, transition quality, and hedonic preference, and neither cedar nor amberwood is named before rose. If T2 ties or wins, remove Azarbre. If T3 wins, omit the added background rather than adding still more base material. The Rose de Mai omission study is canceled while that stock is depleted; no historical arm authorizes future Rose de Mai dosing.

## Claim Ceiling

This document is a **target-linked formulation hypothesis and controlled experiment design**. Inventory presence, ppm, ODT, composite-natural OAV, vapor-pressure modeling, family gates, and temporal simulation are diagnostics. They do not establish physical liking, “best ever” status, similarity to any commercial perfume, measured headspace, safety, stability, maceration success, sillage, longevity, or release readiness. Only blinded physical trials and the appropriate safety review can support those claims.

## Research Basis

- Dior Homme Parfum’s official presentation is used only for the high-level iris-over-woods spatial convention: <https://www.dior.com/en_int/beauty/products/dior-homme-parfum-Y0997193.html>
- Published rose-oil profiling supports treating rose oil as a multiconstituent natural rather than a single-molecule input: <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-34972-5>
- GC–olfactometry work supports odor-active constituent reasoning for rose materials: <https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9336667/>

